[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]What you get[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]REDcode for Cyberpunk 2077[/color][/b], the modding workspace: browse the game's archives, add files to a WolvenKit project, edit CR2W files in a tree and property grid, view textures, meshes in 3D and sounds, browse and change the TweakDB, then pack, install and start the game. Built on WolvenKit's own engine, so it reads the files the way WolvenKit does.[/*]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]EDGEdeck[/color][/b], the mod manager: one list of mods and four verbs (add, on/off, deploy, remove). Hardlink deploys you can undo, conflicts shown even inside archives, Nexus Mods sign-in and updates.[/*]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]REDscript IDE[/color][/b]: redscript support with its language server, built in, so code completion and errors work offline from the first start.[/*]
[*][b]The REDnode HUD theme[/b] as the default look: dark, yellow and cyan, made to sit beside the game.[/*]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]REDagent[/color][/b], the AI assistant, powered by Claude: it can write a REDnode mod with you, run it in the live game, read what went wrong, and fix it. [i](Its editor panel is still being built; the in-game assistant already works.)[/i] Claude Code also runs in EDGEkit: install it from the Extensions view.[/*]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]Extensions that keep themselves current[/color].[/b] The built-in EDGE extensions update from Open VSX as soon as their authors release, so you do not wait for a new EDGEkit.[/*]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]Extensions from Open VSX[/color][/b], the open extension registry, so you can add the language tools and helpers you like.[/*]
[*][b][color=#00ffff]Nothing to hunt for[/color].[/b] No list of extensions to install, no settings to copy from a guide. Install, open your mod folder, start.[/*]
[/list]

[b]Coming next:[/b] [b]EDGEmat[/b], a material editor (pick a garment, click a layer, choose a color, see it on the model), and [b]EDGEwave[/b], a sound studio for editing, mixing and re-voicing the game's sounds. Both join EDGEkit and the extension pack when they are released.

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Why one app[/color][/b][/size]

Cyberpunk modding today means a handful of separate tools, each with its own window, its own setup and its own guide. That is a wall for newcomers, and a chore for everyone else. EDGEkit is the other way round: one install that brings the editor, the game's files, the scripting languages and an assistant together, and keeps them in step with each other.

EDGEkit does [b]not[/b] replace the tools you know. WolvenKit, Cyber Engine Tweaks, redscript, TweakXL, ArchiveXL and the rest stay exactly as they are, and EDGEkit works with their files. Use whatever mix suits you.

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]The EDGE platform[/color][/b][/size]

EDGEkit is the front door to the EDGE tools. Each one is also usable on its own:

[list]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDcode]REDcode[/url][/b]: the modding workspace, for EDGEkit or plain VS Code. In preview on the Marketplace and Open VSX.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/EDGEdeck]EDGEdeck[/url][/b]: the mod manager. In preview on the Marketplace and Open VSX, and in daily use.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDnode]REDnode[/url][/b]: write Cyberpunk mods in JavaScript and TypeScript, with web pages drawn over the game as windows and HUD widgets.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDagent]REDagent[/url][/b]: the AI assistant inside the game, powered by Claude.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDbridge]REDbridge[/url][/b]: the engine room. It runs WolvenKit's libraries as a separate program so the other tools can read and write the game's files.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/EDGEmat]EDGEmat[/url][/b]: the material editor. In development.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/EDGEwave]EDGEwave[/url][/b]: the sound studio. In development.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/EDGEbuilder]EDGEbuilder[/url][/b]: a world builder that runs inside the game on REDnode: browse, drop, position, bake. In development.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Roadmap[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]K0 Decide[/b]: the name, Windows x64 first, the Code - OSS release to build on (1.140.0), updates, no Copilot. Done.[/*]
[*][b]Extension pack[/b]: the EDGE extensions in one install for VS Code and VSCodium. Published as a preview.[/*]
[*][b]K1 Build[/b]: Code - OSS built from source, unbranded. [color=#19f3ff]In progress[/color][/*]
[*][b]K2 Brand[/b]: the EDGEkit name, icons and default theme everywhere. It never says VS Code, and it keeps its data apart from an installed VS Code.[/*]
[*][b]K3 Bundle[/b]: REDcode, EDGEdeck, the HUD theme, REDscript IDE and the other released EDGE extensions built in, working with no network, and updating themselves from Open VSX.[/*]
[*][b]K4 Release[/b]: a Windows installer, published here and on GitHub. EDGEkit updates itself, too.[/*]
[*][b]K5 Track upstream[/b]: newer Code - OSS releases picked up every month or so, sooner for security fixes.[/*]
[/list]

The plan in full: [url=https://edgemodders.github.io/EDGEkit/design.html]EDGEkit design[/url].

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Requirements[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit. (Linux, for Steam Deck modders, may come later.)[/*]
[*]Cyberpunk 2077 on PC, for the game's files.[/*]
[*]For the in-game features: REDnode, and the frameworks it lists. EDGEdeck installs them.[/*]
[*]For REDagent: your own Anthropic API key. Each answer costs you a little; nothing is charged by EDGEmodders.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Questions[/color][/b][/size]

[b]Is this VS Code?[/b]
No. It is built from Code - OSS, the open-source (MIT) project that VS Code is also made from, the same way VSCodium is. EDGEkit is not made by or affiliated with Microsoft, and it does not use the Visual Studio Code name or logo.

[b]Can I keep my VS Code?[/b]
Yes. EDGEkit has its own name, its own settings folder and its own extensions, and does not touch an installed VS Code. If you would rather stay in VS Code, install the EDGEkit extension pack there instead.

[b]How do updates work?[/b]
The EDGE extensions update themselves from Open VSX whenever they are released. A new EDGEkit installer is only needed when the editor itself changes, for example a newer Code - OSS release, so you are not asked to reinstall every few days.

[b]Is Copilot included?[/b]
No. EDGEkit's build leaves Copilot out. Its assistant is REDagent, and Claude Code can be installed from Open VSX if you want it.

[b]Why Open VSX and not the VS Code Marketplace?[/b]
The Marketplace may only be used by Microsoft's own products. Open VSX is the open registry that VSCodium and other editors built on Code - OSS use. The EDGE extensions are published on both.

[b]Does it send telemetry?[/b]
EDGEkit's build turns telemetry off.

[b]Is it free?[/b]
Yes, and open source under the MIT license. The code is on [url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/EDGEkit]GitHub[/url].

[b]Single-player only?[/b]
Yes. Cyberpunk 2077 is a single-player game, and the EDGE tools are for single-player modding.

